\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c6cb332-318e-5ab0-89ce-ace167bccb04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431858 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot (3)+3\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 4=-17\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-3)=-2 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 4=-31\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431859 #edb/ref 1071 "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adc7f30b-f5f4-5c71-84b9-9c4c7b9ab1ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431859 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-5)=20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 10+1\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0=15\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431860 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc37a677-a8ea-5147-882f-3adaa2a20db5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431860 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3}$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form   \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
